blurb: |

  <p>

    The 970 sat near the top of Trek's steel mountain bike range in the '90s. It was one of the last 
    steel frames to be welded in Waterloo, Wisconsin before Trek moved all production to Asia. The first order 
    of business was swapping the original RockShox Quadra fork. The elastomers in those forks rarely age well,
    and I felt the Sunlite black rigid fork gave it a much cleaner look. I splurged a bit on the Cane Creek Forty 
    headset, but I think with the Uno stem and PRO LT riser bars it gives the cockpit a much more modern feel.
  </p>
    <div class="blurb__images">
    <img src="/images/close_ups/trek_970/trek_970_progress.png" alt="Trek 970 as it arrived, with the original RockShox Quadra fork" loading="lazy" data-enlarge>
    <img src="/images/close_ups/trek_970/PXL_20261114_140541141.jpg" alt="Made in USA sticker on the Trek 970 seat tube" loading="lazy" data-enlarge>
  </div>
  <p>
    I simplified the drivetrain with a single 40-tooth Wolf Tooth narrow wide chainring running to
    a 7 speed 11-28 cassette through a Deore XT rear derailleur. The gear range is admittedly narrow, but it should be plenty
    for a commuter bike that will see harsh weather in a flat city. I went with my favorite 26-inch tires, the Schwalbe Billy Bonkers.
    I find it handles the potholes exceptionally well, rolls fast in the city, and provides a nice contrast to the silver. At Mead Street Cycles we're always
    trying to balance aesthetics with practicality and low lifetime maintenance cost. I'm especially proud of this build, and even prouder that I know it will
    get a lot of use in the coming years after a period of being dormant.
  </p>

